  <article style="max-width: 680px; margin: 3rem auto; padding: 0 1rem; line-height: 1.7;">
    <p style="text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.2em; font-size: 0.8rem; color: #888;">
      Field Notes
    </p>
    <h1>Case File 001: The &ldquo;Sam Davis&rdquo; Call</h1>
    <p style="color: #888; font-size: 0.9rem;">Late September 2026 &middot; Names and identifying details changed</p>

    <hr />

    <p>
      An elderly customer called in to check on a new order — a flagship phone,
      512GB, Cosmic Orange, bought outright not long before. Something in her
      story didn&rsquo;t sit right, so I asked her to walk me through it.
    </p>
    <p>
      After the phone was delivered and set up, she got a call from a
      non-carrier number. The caller identified himself only as
      &ldquo;Sam Davis.&rdquo; He knew details about her account — enough that
      she trusted him. He told her she had received the <em>wrong device</em>
      and instructed her to ship the phone back through UPS, on her own dime,
      to an undisclosed location.
    </p>
    <p>
      When she tried to call the number back, it was disconnected.
    </p>
    <p>
      I told her straight: that was a scam. Then I filed a fraud report.
    </p>

    <h2>Why this one stuck with me</h2>
    <p>
      This pattern has been surfacing a lot lately — fake carrier rep, a
      callback number that goes dark, a victim told to mail an expensive device
      to nowhere. But this case had a wrinkle that kills the easy explanation.
      She&rsquo;d only been a customer for about six months. The old breach
      dumps can&rsquo;t contain her account. So where did he get her details?
    </p>
    <p>
      That&rsquo;s the question that decides the theory: an insider with
      account access, a newer breach nobody&rsquo;s talking about yet, or
      order details phished straight from her inbox. The answer lives in one
      detail — <em>exactly</em> what &ldquo;Sam Davis&rdquo; knew before she
      told him anything.
    </p>
    <p>
      The report&rsquo;s filed. It&rsquo;s out of my hands now. But I&rsquo;m
      keeping the file open in my head.
    </p>

    <hr />
    <p style="color: #888; font-size: 0.9rem; font-style: italic;">
      Field notes are anonymized accounts of real cases. No customer PII, no
      internal systems, no exceptions.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
